<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Safi al-Din Ardabili</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1252">1252</a>–1334</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Safi al-Din Ardabili (1252–1334) was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> master from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardabil">Ardabil</a> in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safaviyya">Safaviyya order</a>. His shrine in Ardabil became a major religious centre. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid dynasty</a> took its name from him, and accounts of the Safavid Empire trace the empire's origins to the order he established.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/safi-al-din-ardabili.jpg" alt="Safi al-Din Ardabili" width="320" height="221" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shaykh_Safi_al-Din_interpreting_for_his_disciples_various_verses_by_distinguished_poets_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Safi al-Din Ishaq Ardabili was born in 1252/3 in the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardabil">Ardabil</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, then under Mongol rule. It was a commercial centre in a mountainous area near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>. His father was Amin al-Din Jibrail and his mother Dawlati. The family was of Kurdish origin and spoke Persian. His father's life is obscure: Ibn Bazzaz says he died when Safi was six, while Hayati Tabrizi gives his years as 1216 to 1287.</p><p>The earliest known record of his lineage, the Safwat al-Safa by Ibn Bazzaz, traces his genealogy to Fehruz, a Kurd from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanjan">Sanjan</a>. Vladimir Minorsky refers to Safi's claim to descend from Ali ibn Abu Talib but is uncertain of it. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a> later fabricated a genealogy making him a descendant of the Seventh Twelver Shia Imam.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>According to hagiographical chronicles Safi saw visions of angels as a child. Unable to find a spiritual guide who satisfied him, he left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a> at 20, in 1271/2, intending to meet Shaykh Najib al-Din Buzghush, who died before he arrived. He continued his search in the Caspian region and in 1276/7 met Zahed Gilani at the village of Hilya Karin. He became Zahed's disciple and married his daughter Bibi Fatima, while Zahed's son Hajji Shams al-Din Muhammad married Safi's daughter.</p><p>Zahed named Safi his successor, and Safi led the order from 1301, when Zahed died, though Zahed's family and some followers resented it. He renamed the Zahediyeh the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safaviyya">Safaviyya</a> and reformed it from a local order into a religious movement whose propaganda reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> and even <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>. He gained considerable political influence and named his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadr">Sadr</a> al-Din Musa his heir, which shows his resolve to keep leadership in his family. He wrote poems in Old Azeri and Persian, of which only a few double verses, called Dobaytis, survive.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Safi died on 12 September 1334 and was buried. He was the eponymous ancestor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid dynasty</a>, which ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> from 1501 to 1736. His poems are of linguistic importance, since B. V. Miller concluded that the dialect of the quatrains was the language of the ancestors of the modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talysh">Talysh</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Safi-ad-Din Ardabili” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It traced its spiritual lineage to the Sufi sheikh <b>Safi al-Din Ardabili</b> (1252–1334), from whom the dynasty took its name.</blockquote><a href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardabil">Ardabil</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardabil">Ardabil</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.25,48.3&amp;year=1305" aria-label="Ardabil on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5752 790 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Safi al-Din Ardabili"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6202 1060h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6202 1060h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.25,48.3&amp;year=1305">What was here in 1305? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
